## 2. Architecture Assessment

### Tech Stack — Strong Choices

| Choice | Assessment |
|---|---|
| Next.js 14 App Router | ✅ Correct. Server components + API routes in one project. Vercel-native. |
| TypeScript strict mode | ✅ Essential for a project of this scale. |
| Supabase (Postgres + RLS + Storage + Auth) | ✅ Excellent. One service handles DB, auth, storage, and edge functions. Free tier is generous. |
| Tailwind + shadcn/ui | ✅ Fast UI development. shadcn is flexible and unstyled — good for customisation. |
| Zustand | ✅ Lightweight, no boilerplate. Perfect for language toggle, sidebar state, user context. |
| Zod | ✅ Runtime validation on API routes is critical when AI outputs JSON. |
| pnpm | ✅ Fast, disk-efficient. Good choice. |

### AI Architecture — Well-Designed, One Concern

The **three-tier fallback chain** (Groq → OpenAI → Claude) is smartly designed:

```
Groq (free, fast) → OpenAI (reliable) → Claude (best Nepali)
```

- Retry logic on 429/503/timeout is correct
- `aiChat()` as a single entry point is clean
- Logging fallback events to `ai_logs` enables production monitoring

> [!WARNING]
> **Cost concern:** If Groq's free tier hits limits consistently, OpenAI and Anthropic will start billing. With 50 requests/user/day and multiple users, fallback costs could escalate without a billing alert system. The spec doesn't mention a cost monitoring or alerting mechanism.

### OpenMAIC Integration — High Risk, High Reward

The spec proposes a **full merge** (not iframe) of the Tsinghua OpenMAIC project. This is architecturally correct (avoids cross-origin pain), but comes with risks:

| Aspect | Assessment |
|---|---|
| Merge approach | ✅ Direct component import is the right call vs. iframe |
| Estimated changes (~80 lines) | ⚠️ Optimistic. Dependency conflicts and config merging will add friction. |
| OpenMAIC maintenance | ⚠️ Once forked and modified, upstream updates become hard to merge |
| Groq as OpenAI-compatible backend | ✅ Clever. `OPENAI_BASE_URL` swap is clean. |

