## 5. Risk Analysis

### 🔴 Critical Risks

| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| **Scope creep / burnout** | 34 build steps as a solo dev. High chance of abandonment past step 15. | Phase ruthlessly. Ship steps 1–12 as MVP. Everything else is Phase 2+. |
| **Groq free tier limits** | 14,400 req/day sounds high but with AI assistant, quiz, planner, risk assessor, interview all making calls — a power user could hit 50 personal limit fast. | The per-user 50/day limit is smart. Monitor aggregate usage too. |
| **OpenMAIC merge complexity** | The "~80 lines" estimate is almost certainly low. Dependency conflicts (React versions, Tailwind config, etc.) could take days. | Attempt the merge early (step 7 in build order is correct). If it fails, fall back to iframe as Plan B. |

### 🟠 Moderate Risks

| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| **AI output parsing** | Groq/OpenAI returning malformed JSON for syllabus extraction or quiz generation. | Zod validation is specified. Add retry with a "fix your JSON" prompt. |
| **Bilingual testing** | Every feature needs Nepali testing. Easy to ship broken NP UI. | Automated screenshot tests in both languages. |
| **Whisper Nepali accuracy** | Groq Whisper `ne` language support quality is uncertain. | Test early. Have a "type instead" fallback always visible. |
| **nepali-date-converter reliability** | Community npm package for BS dates. May have edge cases. | Pin the version. Write unit tests for known date pairs. |

### 🟢 Low Risks

| Risk | Notes |
|---|---|
| Supabase scaling | Free tier is generous. RLS + Postgres are battle-tested. |
| Vercel deployment | Straightforward Next.js deploy. No special config needed. |
| shadcn/ui compatibility | Actively maintained, works perfectly with Next.js 14. |

